</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yu Meng
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Virginia
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">yumeng5@virginia.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) for large language models (LLMs) relies on expensive human-annotated datasets, while Reinforcement Learning from AI Feedback (RLAIF) also incurs significant costs, requiring the collection of diverse prompts and corresponding responses, often necessitating external reward models or proprietary models like GPT-4 to annotate preference pairs.
In this work, we introduce Self-Alignment Optimization (SAO), a fully self-synthetic framework for LLM alignment, where all training data, including prompts (<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, user queries), responses, and preferences, are generated by the model itself.
Specifically, SAO first instructs the LLM to engage in persona role-play and generate diverse prompts and responses, which are then self-evaluated for preference optimization.
Extensive experiments demonstrate that SAO effectively enhances the model’s chat capabilities on standard benchmarks like AlpacaEval 2.0, while maintaining strong performance on downstream objective tasks (<span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, question-answering, math reasoning).
Our work provides a practical solution for self-improvement in aligning LLMs, and the code for reproducing our results is available at:
<a href="https://github.com/SJY8460/SAO" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/SJY8460/SAO</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have revolutionized the field of natural language processing (NLP), demonstrating remarkable capabilities in tasks such as mathematical reasoning, code generation, and dialogue generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bubeck et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A key advancement in LLMs is their alignment with human preference to create more helpful and reliable assistants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mishra et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Victor et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thoppilan et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Common approaches include supervised fine-tuning (SFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tunstall et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, based on human-demonstration pairs, and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF)  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stiennon et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which leverages signals from human preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, collecting demonstrations and preference labels is a expensive, time-consuming process, involving substantial human annotating efforts.
To address this challenge, reinforcement learning from AI feedback (RLAIF) has been gaining attention, where a reward model is trained using AI-labeled preference data or directly by LLMs through specially designed prompts to annotate preference pairs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
However, RLAIF remains costly, typically requiring strong, proprietary models (<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, GPT-4) or specialized reward model designs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to work effectively.
Moreover, these approaches often incur additional overhead from data filtering to obtain the final clean dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Recent self-improvement approaches for LLM alignment, such as Self-Rewarding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and SPPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, have demonstrated promising results. However, these approaches are not fully self-synthetic and still require external intervention: Self-Rewarding relies on human-labeled data as few-shot templates to generate synthetic data and then mixes both human and synthetic data for model optimization, while SPPO requires prompts from existing datasets and external reward models for preference labeling. Such dependencies limit their scalability and accessibility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we propose a fully self-synthetic method for LLM alignment, termed Self-Alignment Optimization (SAO), eliminating the need for costly training data collection and annotation.
Drawing inspiration from the compress-and-decompress approach to world knowledge from a persona perspective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tseng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> and the success of self-improvement mechanisms  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samuel, 2000</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, SAO begins with (1) instructing the LLM to engage in persona role-play and generate diverse prompts (<span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, user queries).
(2) The LLM then generates paired responses for each prompt, which will be ranked through self-judgment.
(3) Lastly, preference optimization is performed to fine-tune the model based on the ranked responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amini et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Across multiple standard chat benchmarks for LLM alignment, SAO demonstrates substantial performance improvements compared to the backbone model.
For example, on AlpacaEval 2.0, SAO boosts the Length-Controlled Win Rate (LC) and Win Rate (WR) of Gemma-2-9B-it by 18.1% and 27.9%, respectively.
On MT-Bench, SAO improves the average score of Gemma-2-9B-it from 8.41 to 8.66 points. On Arena-Hard, SAO also boosts the model’s WR from 40.8% to 54.3%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, we find that SAO maintains or even enhances the backbone LLM’s performance on downstream objective NLP tasks, though the model was not trained using domain-specific data.
In the evaluation of the Open LLM Leaderboard, SAO enables Gemma-2-9B-it to achieve an average score of 74.41 across all benchmarks, surpassing its baseline score of 74.28, without any further training.
We highlight that this improvement is significant, as models trained on manually crafted datasets often enhance alignment at the cost of compromised general capability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
For instance, Gemma-2-9B-it trained on UltraFeedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cui et al., 2024</a>)</cite> achieves only a score of 70.38 using the same training algorithm as SAO. These results highlight SAO’s effectiveness in synthesizing high-quality data that enhances a model’s subject-specific capabilities while preserving its downstream performance in a more data-efficient manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand the empirical results, we further conduct an in-depth analysis with the Gemma-2-9B-it model. Specifically, we compare the LLM’s self-synthesized prompts with manually crafted prompts from UltraFeedback. Interestingly, we find that synthetic prompts lead to significantly better performance than those from the Ultrafeedback benchmark when followed by the same self-improvement process in SAO (<span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, Step 2 and 3), with a 16.46% improvement in WR.
This emphasizes the importance of prompt construction for LLM alignment.
We also find that SAO’s effectiveness largely stems from the surprisingly strong self-judging ability of the backbone model, which even surpasses the external reward model ArmoRM-Llama3-8B-v0.1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and even GPT4-level feedback when used within the SAO framework.
This is further confirmed by the findings in Section <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS0.Px5" title="Evaluation of feedback sources. ‣ 4.4 Analysis and Ablations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, which show that the model is robust to various judgment criteria designs, indicating that the model’s strong evaluation ability,
thereby contributing to the effectiveness of SAO.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are summarized as follows: (1) We propose a self-alignment optimization framework, which aligns the model with its own generated prompts and feedback as preferences, eliminating the need for expensive data collection and annotation.
(2) On multiple standard chat benchmarks, the LLMs trained with SAO achieve competitive results compared to strong proprietary models like GPT-4 and state-of-the-art open-weight models trained on expensively annotated external datasets. (3) In contrast to previous resource-intensive approaches, SAO enhances LLM alignment while maintaining strong downstream objective task performance, providing a practical and scalable solution for improving a model’s chat abilities in a more balanced and effective manner.
(4) We demonstrate that the SAO framework exhibits long-term scaling properties in both synthetic dataset size and iterative optimization, providing a sustainable path for continued model improvement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Synthetic Data for LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of fine-tuning LLMs, human-crafted data has proven remarkably effective, significantly enhancing performance on tasks like code generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Roziere et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and mathematical reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. While human-generated data is typically of high quality, acquiring sufficient amounts is often prohibitively expensive. Consequently, the use of synthetic data has gained popularity as a cost-effective substitute for human data. This approach primarily leverages advanced LLMs, such as the GPT series <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radford et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2023</a>)</cite>, to generate high-quality data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Josifoski et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Taori et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chiang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>.
Recent studies have also emphasized the benefits of using LLMs’ rephrasing capabilities to improve prompt responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prasad et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, as well as augmenting synthetic data for more effective fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Unlike prior research, which typically relies on more advanced models for generating synthetic training data during, our approach directly generates synthetic data from the target model itself, streamlining the process and reducing dependency on external resources.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Persona Roleplay</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Persona roleplay in LLMs can be viewed as a compress-and-decompress mechanism for world knowledge. In this framework, world knowledge is compressed into distributed representations, which are then decompressed by various personas to generate texts based on their unique knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Delétang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. Recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al. (2024a)</a></cite> introduced the <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Persona Hub</span>, a system automatically constructed from vast web data. This hub enables the exploration of most perspectives embedded within LLMs, facilitating the creation of diverse synthetic data at scale, without the need for seed corpora.
In this work, we directly leverage personas from this hub, which can be seamlessly integrated into customized data synthesis prompts, leveraging the LLM’s strong role-playing capabilities and offering exceptional versatility.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>LLM-as-a-Judge</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using LLM-as-a-Judge prompting to evaluate language models has become a standard approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubois et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fernandes et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saha et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. This technique is not only employed for evaluation but also for training reward models and curating data, as mentioned in prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. While some studies focus on creating training data to enhance an LLM’s performance as a judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, our approach uniquely integrates this judging capability with general instruction-following skills, setting it apart from existing methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Self-Alignment Optimization (SAO)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
              
              
             Base model <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, number of personas <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, preference optimization algorithm <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
              
              
             Optimized model <math id="alg1.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             Initialize personas templates <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{1},r_{2},\ldots,r_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             Initialize dataset <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,2,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,2,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{0}}(r_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{0}}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">§ <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Diverse Prompt Generation ‣ 3 Self-Alignment Optimization ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{0}}(\cdot\mid x_{\text{prompt}}^{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}\leftarrow\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{0}}(\cdot\mid x_{\text{prompt}}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">§ <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Pairwise Response Generation ‣ 3 Self-Alignment Optimization ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(y_{\text{w}},y_{\text{l}})\leftarrow\mathcal{R}_{\theta_{0}}(y_{1},y_{2}\mid x_{\text{prompt}}^{i},x_{\text{rank}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>w</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>l</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rank</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{\text{w}},y_{\text{l}})\leftarrow\mathcal{R}_{\theta_{0}}(y_{1},y_{2}\mid x_{\text{prompt}}^{i},x_{\text{rank}})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">§ <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Self-Judgment ‣ 3 Self-Alignment Optimization ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
              <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\leftarrow\mathcal{D}\cup\{(x_{\text{prompt}}^{i},y_{\text{w}},y_{\text{l}})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>w</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>l</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\leftarrow\mathcal{D}\cup\{(x_{\text{prompt}}^{i},y_{\text{w}},y_{\text{l}})\}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;">§ <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4 Dataset Construction ‣ 3 Self-Alignment Optimization ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             Optimize: <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{1}\leftarrow\argmin_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{A}}(\mathcal{M}_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{1}\leftarrow\argmin_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{\mathcal{A}}(\mathcal{M}_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Return</span> <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\theta_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Self-Alignment Optimization</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present the overview of Self-Alignment Optimization (SAO) in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.3 LLM-as-a-Judge ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and elaborate on its design in the following sections.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Diverse Prompt Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To facilitate a comprehensive range of training scenarios, we utilize an LLM denoted as <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, for the generation of diverse prompts tailored to specific persona roles, as depicted in Figure <a href="#A4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix D Prompt Template ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (top). Given a set of role templates <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}=\{r_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}=\{r_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we derive a unique prompt for each persona:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}=\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(r_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}=\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(r_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this context, <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the prompt generated for the <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th persona <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure the diversity of generated prompts, we impose a constraint such that each persona can generate only a single question. The persona resources are randomly sampled from Persona-Hub <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ge et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Pairwise Response Generation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each generated prompt, we create a pair of responses to enable comparative evaluation. Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of prompts and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> the space of responses. For each prompt <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we generate two responses <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}\in\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}\in\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> using the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}\sim\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x_{\text{prompt}}^{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}\sim\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x_{\text{prompt}}^{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Generating additional responses could potentially yield better performance but would increase computational costs and evaluation time. We leave this exploration for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Self-Judgment</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the quality of generated responses, we implement a self-judgment mechanism. This process entails the LLM evaluating its own outputs, thereby simulating human preferences. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#A4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix D Prompt Template ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> (bottom), we query the LLM with a ranking prompt <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{rank}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rank</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{rank}}</annotation></semantics></math> to compare the responses <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> based on their relevance and quality relative to <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(y_{\text{w}},y_{\text{l}})=\mathcal{R}_{\theta}(y_{1},y_{2}\mid x_{\text{prompt}}^{i},x_{\text{rank}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>w</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>l</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rank</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{\text{w}},y_{\text{l}})=\mathcal{R}_{\theta}(y_{1},y_{2}\mid x_{\text{prompt}}^{i},x_{\text{rank}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>w</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{\text{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mtext>l</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{\text{l}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the superior and inferior responses, respectively. The function <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> encapsulates the LLM’s decision-making process in ranking the responses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Dataset Construction</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a synthetic dataset <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> by aggregating the generated prompts and ranked responses for each persona:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(x_{\text{prompt}}^{i},y_{\text{win}}^{i},y_{\text{lose}}^{i})\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mtext>win</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mtext>lose</mtext><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(x_{\text{prompt}}^{i},y_{\text{win}}^{i},y_{\text{lose}}^{i})\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of personas. This dataset forms the cornerstone of our preference optimization process, allowing the model to learn from its own generated and ranked responses across diverse personas.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Preference Optimization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advancements in preference optimization have demonstrated significant potential in aligning LLMs with human preferences. Techniques such as Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and Simple Preference Optimization (SimPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have gained prominence due to their efficacy in fine-tuning LLMs to better reflect human preferences.
In this study, we employ SimPO due to its suitability for our dataset, which frequently contains longer responses. Its length normalization technique effectively captures nuanced information at the token level, making it particularly well-suited to our requirements and we provide a more detailed analysis and comparison of these methods in Section <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS0.Px3" title="Different optimization algorithms. ‣ 4.4 Analysis and Ablations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">SimPO introduces a length-normalized reward formulation that aligns with the likelihood metric guiding generation with a scaling constant <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}r(x,y)&amp;=\frac{\beta}{|y|}\log\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(y\mid x)\\
&amp;=\frac{\beta}{|y|}\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}\log\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(y_{i}\mid x,y_{&lt;i})\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}r(x,y)&amp;=\frac{\beta}{|y|}\log\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(y\mid x)\\
&amp;=\frac{\beta}{|y|}\sum_{i=1}^{|y|}\log\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(y_{i}\mid x,y_{&lt;i})\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Additionally, it incorporates a target reward margin <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure a minimum difference between the rewards of winning and losing responses:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(y_{w}\succ y_{l}\mid x)=\sigma\left(r(x,y_{w})-r(x,y_{l})-\gamma\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(y_{w}\succ y_{l}\mid x)=\sigma\left(r(x,y_{w})-r(x,y_{l})-\gamma\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The overall objective is then formulated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{M}_{\theta})=&amp;-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})\sim\mathcal{D}}\biggl[\log\sigma\biggl(\frac{\beta}{|y_{w}|}\log\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(y_{w}|x)\\
&amp;\hskip 20.00003pt-\frac{\beta}{|y_{l}|}\log\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(y_{l}|x)-\gamma\biggr)\biggr]\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mi>log</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mi>log</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}(\mathcal{M}_{\theta})=&amp;-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})\sim\mathcal{D}}\biggl[\log\sigma\biggl(\frac{\beta}{|y_{w}|}\log\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(y_{w}|x)\\
&amp;\hskip 20.00003pt-\frac{\beta}{|y_{l}|}\log\mathcal{M}_{\theta}(y_{l}|x)-\gamma\biggr)\biggr]\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p5.1" class="ltx_p">It guides the optimization process, enabling the model to learn from self-generated preferences and improve the alignment with desired outcomes.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparative analysis of various baseline models and our proposed SAO method using AlpacaEval 2.0. The table presents Length-Controlled Win Rate (LC), Win Rate (WR), and Standard Deviation (STD) for each model, evaluated against GPT-4-Turbo-1106 and Qwen2-72B-Instruct. We also provide additional judge analysis in Section <a href="#S4.SS4.SSS0.Px6" title="Quality of generation vs. judging. ‣ 4.4 Analysis and Ablations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:310.5pt;height:89.1pt;vertical-align:-43.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-248.7pt,71.4pt) scale(0.384374153877958,0.384374153877958) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" rowspan="3"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Model</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="6">AlpacaEval 2.0</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="3">GPT-4-Turbo-1106</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="3">Qwen2-72B-Instruct</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">LC (%)</td>
