</section>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-judge evaluation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further validate the robustness of SAO improvements and address potential evaluation bias, we evaluated our models using multiple independent judge models beyond the default GPT-4 and Qwen2-72B-instruct judges in AlpacaEval 2.0. Specifically, we employed three diverse state-of-the-art LLM evaluators: LLaMA-3.3-70B, LLaMA-3.1-70B, and Qwen-2.5-72B to evaluate models trained with self-feedback and LLaMA-feedback.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ Multi-judge evaluation. ‣ 4.4 Analysis and Ablations ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Aligning Large Language Models via Fully Self-Synthetic Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, Gemma-2-9B-it with self-feedback demonstrates consistent and substantial improvements across all three judges. With LLaMA-3.3-70B as the evaluator, WR increases from 45.22% to 73.11%; with LLaMA-3.1-70B, from 42.66% to 69.50%; and with Qwen-2.5-72B, from 42.61% to 76.09%. The LC metric also shows steady gains across all evaluators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, SAO trained with LLaMA-feedback consistently underperforms the self-feedback approach, with WR ranging from 32.67% to 38.39% across different judges. These results confirm that the performance gains from our SAO method are robust and generalize across different evaluation perspectives, demonstrating genuine alignment improvements rather than judge-specific overfitting.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Multi-judge evaluation on AlpacaEval 2.0 showing consistent improvements across different evaluators.</figcaption>
